# The openOODA Registry

File: ooda/opm/docs/REGISTRY.oot

This document describes how the openOODA registry works and how opm talks to it. The registry is the public catalog of packages. opm uses it for `search` and `add` when a project does not have a local vendor.

## 1. The Default Registry

The default registry is `https://registry.openooda.org`. opm refuses any other origin unless the user lists it with `ooda opm reg <url>`. The host check is strict: only `https`, no `@`, no spaces, no lookalikes, no path traversal in the URL.

## 2. CAS Integrity

Every package is identified by the SHA-256 of its payload. The hash is the CAS (Content-Addressable Store) id. opm stores the payload at `.ooda_modules/cas/<sha>/<payload-name>`. The same bytes always produce the same id. There is no name-based lookup; the sha is the only key.

This means:

- A package cannot be substituted. A different payload has a different sha.
- Two packages with the same name and different versions are different ids.
- A registry that returns a payload with a sha different from its manifest fails the install.

## 3. The Catalog

A registry serves a flat text file called `catalog`. Each line is one package version, in `name|version|caps|deps|sha` form. The line ends with `\n`. The catalog is read once per command. opm does not hold a long-lived connection.

Example:

```
cli-helper|0.4.1|FsRead,FsWrite,Http|json-parser@^2.0.0,http-client@~1.5.0|5d41402abc...
json-parser|2.1.0|FsRead,FsWrite| |e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855
http-client|1.5.4|FsRead,FsWrite,Net| |5d41402abc4b2a76b9719d911017c592a2b5a8e0c6b7c4e8a3c2d3a4b5c6d7e8
```

## 4. Capability Declarations

Each catalog line lists the caps the package needs. opm reads this and refuses the install if the project does not have the cap in its allow-list. The list is the full set: any cap missing from the allow-list is a hard fail.

A registry cannot grant a cap. The project owner decides the allow-list, not the registry. This is the core of the openOODA supply chain model: a package cannot widen the set of resources it touches just by being installed.

## 5. Zero Ambient Supply Chain Security

opm has no ambient trust. There is no global allow-list, no pre-installed keyring, and no fallback registry. Every install is verified against a list the user wrote in `ooda.pkg` (or the project default). Every payload is verified against the `sha256` field in the manifest. Every signature is verified against a secret the user controls.

This means:

- A registry outage does not compromise a build. Local installs work without network.
- A compromised registry cannot ship a different payload. The sha fails.
- A new package version is not picked up unless the user updates the range.
- A yanked version is refused even if the catalog still lists it. The `yank` file wins.

## 6. Search

`ooda opm search <query>` walks every configured registry and prints packages whose name contains the query as a substring. The empty query lists every package. The output is one line per hit: `name version caps store [pay=...]`.

The query is a substring. opm does not support regex, fuzzy match, or faceted search. The catalog is small; substring is enough.

## 7. Install

`ooda opm add <name>` runs through these steps:

1. Walk the configured registries. Get the catalog.
2. Pick the highest version of `<name>` that satisfies the range in `ooda.pkg`.
3. Walk the transitive deps. Apply the same picker to each.
4. Resolve conflicts. The default resolver is single-pass. `--pubgrub` opts in to backtracking.
5. For each picked node, fetch the payload and verify the sha against the manifest.
6. Verify the signature if the manifest has one.
7. Write the payload to CAS, the file vendor, and the lock.

Any failure in steps 2-7 is a hard fail. No partial install.

## 8. Verification

`ooda opm audit` (or `ooda opm sbom`) walks the current lock and reports any hit against:

- The vendor `yank` file (a line `name@version` or bare `name`).
- The vendor `advisory` file (a line that starts with `name@version`).
- The std/oodac pin header (a toolchain change since the lock was written).

The audit is local. It does not call the registry.

## 9. Publish

`ooda opm publish` writes a package to a local registry tree. The default is `.ooda-cache/registry`. The output is `<root>/<name>/<version>/` with `ooda.pkg`, the payload, and a signed `index` file. The user can `rsync` the tree to a real server, or extend the dispatcher to PUT when the runtime gains an HTTP upload primitive.

## 10. The Local-Registry Model

opm ships a local-registry simulation because the runtime has no `fetch_put` primitive. The `publish` subcommand does not make an HTTP call. It writes to disk. This is by design: every step of the supply chain is reproducible from a directory tree, with no daemon, no secret store, and no daemon key.
